@page "/projects/quiz"

<ProjectPage>

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        My take on Kahoot's classroom quiz: a host starts a game on a shared screen, players
        join on their own phones with a room code, everyone sees each question at the same
        moment, and answers come back instantly.
    </p>

    <p>
        It's <strong>deliberately unfinished</strong>, and I'd rather say so up front. I built
        it to learn three things: Next.js, a popular toolkit for building websites, before an
        internship; how to keep many people's screens in sync; and how to put a site online
        myself, while studying for Amazon's AWS Cloud Practitioner certificate. Once I'd
        learned those, polishing it further wasn't the point.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping games apart</h2>

    <p>
        The interesting part is running several games at once without them getting mixed up.
        Every player should see their own game's question at the same moment as everyone else
        in it, and nothing from anyone else's game. The room code is what ties each player to
        the right game.
    </p>

    <p>
        That made it a good learning project. An ordinary website only sends you something
        when you ask for it, by loading a page or clicking, which wouldn't have taught me
        anything new. Here the server has to send each question to every player's screen at
        the same moment, which is a genuinely different problem.
    </p>

    <h2>Putting it online myself</h2>

    <p>
        I put it online on a server rented from <strong>Amazon Web Services</strong>, with a
        tool that restarts it if it crashes. I checked that it worked, then took it down. It
        was a demo, never meant to stay live. Setting up a server and getting something
        running on it taught me far more about what cloud providers do for you than the
        certificate course did on its own.
    </p>

    <p>
        It's my earliest and simplest project, and it shows next to what I build now: no
        automated tests, no automatic quality checks on changes, and checking what players
        type still listed as a "future improvement" in the project's notes. I've left it that
        way rather than quietly going back to fix it.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
